---
import PublicationLayout from '../../layouts/PublicationLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'availability', label: 'What is available' },
  { id: 'release-status', label: 'Release status' },
  { id: 'public-fixtures', label: 'Try the public fixtures' },
  { id: 'non-claims', label: 'What the verifier does not prove' },
];
---

<PublicationLayout
  category="News"
  type="Product preview"
  title="nomue verifier Release 1 candidate is now public"
  description="An experimental local verifier is available for anyone to inspect and test; it is now pinned to nomue Protocol Release 1."
  published="August 16, 2026"
  updated="August 24, 2026"
  status="Experimental verifier — 0.2.1-rc.0; pinned to Protocol Release 1"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="availability">
    <h2>What is available</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-verifier" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nomue verifier repository</a>
      contains an experimental verifier that anyone can run on their own computer to check
      the supported parts of a nomue Record—a machine-readable file that connects research
      outputs to their evidence. It works independently of the organization that created
      the Record.
    </p>
    <p>
      It checks whether the Record has the required structure, whether its content has
      changed, whether the set of rules it claims to follow is supported, and whether its
      stored Welch test results match a fresh calculation for the Release 1 support target.
      It does not contact a Licklider server or run code embedded in the Record.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="release-status">
    <h2>Release status</h2>
    <div class="notice">
      <p><strong>nomue Protocol Release 1 was published on August 24, 2026.</strong></p>
      <p>
        The verifier repository is now pinned to the published Release 1 identities. The
        verifier package itself remains experimental at <code>0.2.1-rc.0</code>; publication
        of the Protocol does not turn this package into a separate stable verifier release.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      See <a href="/news/nomue-protocol-release-1/">nomue Protocol Release 1 Public Draft</a>
      for the current Protocol release, its scope, and the signed release artifacts.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="public-fixtures">
    <h2>Try the two public fixtures</h2>
    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/licklider-ai/nomue-verifier.git
cd nomue-verifier
npm install

npm exec -- nomue verify records/valid.json --format json
npm exec -- nomue verify records/invalid-result-mismatch.json --format json</code></pre>
    <p>
      The first fixture is expected to pass every check supported by this verifier version.
      The second deliberately contains a result mismatch and is expected to fail the
      relevant check.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="non-claims">
    <h2>What the verifier does not prove</h2>
    <p>Even when every supported check passes, the verifier does not prove that:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the study or its scientific conclusions are valid;</li>
      <li>the input data and researcher statements are true;</li>
      <li>cause-and-effect claims are justified;</li>
      <li>results are correct under versions or settings outside the supported Release 1 bundle; or</li>
      <li>the Record came from Licklider’s production system.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The verifier does not return a single overall <code>VERIFIED</code> label. Read its
      JSON report to see the result of each check and the limits of what was tested.
    </p>
  </section>
</PublicationLayout>
